    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d1046">

                <pb facs="1177" n="1167" xml:id="pg1167"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">711.94114 Mail/72: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Chargé in the Soviet Union</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Hamilton</hi>) <hi
                        rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State
                    </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Moscow</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1944-05-03T11:00:00+03:00">May 3, 1944—11
                            a.m.</date>
                        <lb/> [Received 7:20 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>1555. Pursuant to the Department’s 988, April 21, 10 p.m.,<anchor n="77"
                        xml:id="d1046-f1"/> the subject of shipments of prisoners of war mail by
                    Soviet vessels to Vladivostok was discussed with the new chief of the American
                    Section of the Foreign Office<anchor n="78" xml:id="d1046-f2"/> on April 28 who
                    stated he would look into the matter.</p>
                <p>In a previous discussion of this question another Foreign Office official
                    confidentially expressed the personal view that the Soviet authorities were
                    concerned that the carrying of such mail would cause difficulties with the
                    Japanese. It was pointed out to him that this mail would in any event pass
                    through Japanese hands and that the forwarding of such mail was in accordance
                    with existing agreements between the Japanese and American Governments. The
                    Soviet official stated that nevertheless he thought the Soviet authorities felt
                    that the carrying of such mail might present an excuse for the Japanese to stop
                    Soviet ships which the Soviet Government was anxious to avoid. He would not
                    discuss the matter further and emphasized that he was giving a personal
                    “impression” in confidence.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Hamilton</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d1046-f1" n="77" xml:id="d1046-f1-content">
                <p>Not printed; it instructed the Embassy to take up again the subject of the
                    shipment of mail on Soviet vessels from Pacific ports of the United States to
                    Vladivostok (711.94114 Mail/59).</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d1046-f2" n="78" xml:id="d1046-f2-content">
                <p>Semen Konstantinovich Tsarapkin had replaced Georgy Nikolayevich Zarubin as Chief
                    of the American Section in the People’s Commissariat for Foreign Affairs.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
